{"name":"Romantic Poetry","hex":"#cac0ce","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"romantic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a work of literature, a writer etc.: being like or having the characteristics of a romance, or poetic tale of a mythic or quasi-historical time; fantastic. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Fictitious, imaginary."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person with romantic character (a character like those of the knights in a mythic romance).","A person who is behaving romantically (in a manner befitting someone who feels an idealized form of love)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/romantic"},{"term":"poetry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Literature composed in verse or language exhibiting conscious attention to patterns and rhythm.","A poet's literary production."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/poetry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Romantic poetry","blocks":[{"text":"Romantic poetry is the poetry of the Romantic era, an artistic, literary, musical and intellectual movement that originated in Europe towards the end of the 18th century. It involved a reaction against prevailing Neoclassical ideas of the 18th century, and lasted approximately from 1800 to 1850. Romantic poets rebelled against the style of poetry from the eighteenth century which was based around epics, odes, satires, elegies, epistles and songs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Louis_Edouard_Fournier_-_The_Funeral_of_Shelley_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/330px-Louis_Edouard_Fournier_-_The_Funeral_of_Shelley_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romantic_poetry","wikidata":"Q2735649"},{"title":"Poetry","blocks":[{"text":"Poetry is a form of literary art that uses aesthetic and often rhythmic qualities of language to evoke meanings in addition to, or in place of, literal or surface-level meanings. A particular instance of poetry is a poem and is written by a poet. Poets use a variety of techniques called poetic devices, such as assonance, alliteration, consonance, euphony and cacophony, onomatopoeia, rhythm, rhyme schemes and sound symbolism, to produce musical or other artistic effects. They also frequently organize these devices into poetic structures, which may be strict or loose, conventional or invented by the poet. Poetic structures vary dramatically by language and cultural convention, but they often rely on rhythmic metre: patterns of syllable stress or syllable weight. They may also use repeating patterns of phonemes, phoneme groups, tones, words, or entire phrases. Poetic structures may even be semantic."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poetry","wikidata":"Q482"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Karel_Hynek_Macha_statue.jpeg/500px-Karel_Hynek_Macha_statue.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Karel_Hynek_Macha_statue.jpeg/1280px-Karel_Hynek_Macha_statue.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Karel_Hynek_Macha_statue.jpeg/500px-Karel_Hynek_Macha_statue.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In the Western cultural context, romanticism substantially contributed to the idea of what a real poet should look like. An idealized statue of a Czech man Karel Hynek Mácha (in Petřín Park, Prague) represents him as a slim, tender and perhaps unhealthy boy. However, he had in reality a strong, robust and muscular body. He was the head(literal) of the Romantic Poetry Age or the Age of Romantic Poetry.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKarel_Hynek_Macha_statue.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png/500px-Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png/500px-Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABooks_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg/250px-The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg/330px-The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg/250px-The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wiki